WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Speaker 1: We should be finding out relatively&nbsp;
soon whether Donald Trump is going to be indicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.920
for his role in allegedly trying to overturn the&nbsp;
presidential election results in Georgia back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.320
in 2020. And the person who is saying that&nbsp;
the decision is coming soon is someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.200
would know a thing or two about it. It's Fulton&nbsp;
County District Attorney Fani Willis, who is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.480
Georgia prosecutor, investigating whether Trump&nbsp;
broke election laws in the state by trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:31.440
strong arm Brad Raffensperger and get him to find&nbsp;
the, quote, 11,780 votes. She told the Associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.240
Press that she expects to come to a decision&nbsp;
about whether to indict Trump by June 30th. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.000
that sounds very direct and it definitely sounds&nbsp;
like it's coming soon, especially because we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.040
in the middle of June now. But then we find out&nbsp;
there's a catch in that. That catch is that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.240
actually made the statement back in January. She&nbsp;
said, quote, I certainly think that in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.520
half of the year, that decision will be made.&nbsp;
But who knows if between now and then things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.160
have changed, if the timeline has been moved&nbsp;
back. What is promising, though, is that Willis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.120
has been ramping up her investigation in recent&nbsp;
months. She's interviewed a few dozen people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.920
including Secretary of State Brad Raffensperger.&nbsp;
She was granted approval for a special grand jury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.840
but has held off on having witnesses testify&nbsp;
before the jury. Willis has also commented that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.960
she would want to make the announcement after the&nbsp;
primary elections as to not give the appearance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:27.200
a conflict of interest or trying to influence the&nbsp;
race in any way. And those primary elections took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.760
place a few weeks ago in Georgia. So now around&nbsp;
now seems like the right time to come out with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.760
the announcement. Right. Are you going to indict&nbsp;
him or are you not? Especially because if she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.280
worried about this idea of election interference,&nbsp;
then, you know, you don't want to wait so long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.600
as to run up against the November midterms.&nbsp;
You don't want to wait until right before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.640
the general election either. So I'd imagine it's&nbsp;
probably coming soon to me. For what it's worth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.200
I think that Trump clearly broke the law on this.&nbsp;
I think it's a slam dunk case. If you look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.160
Georgia election law, it says, quote, A person&nbsp;
commits the offense of criminal solicitation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.640
commit election fraud in the first degree when&nbsp;
with intent, then another person engaged in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.960
conduct constituting a felony. Under this article,&nbsp;
he or she solicits requests, commands, importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.400
or otherwise, attempts to cause the other person&nbsp;
to engage in such conduct. And the punishment is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.680
somewhere between one and three years in prison.&nbsp;
For what it's worth, and I'm no lawyer, but if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.360
you take a look at the letter of the law there,&nbsp;
it certainly looks like Donald Trump committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.280
this crime and you at least have enough evidence&nbsp;
to go to an indictment and we'll let the jury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.560
decide whether he is guilty. They also have audio&nbsp;
recordings of him. They can establish that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.080
was a well-planned, well coordinated effort to do&nbsp;
this. It certainly wasn't a one off thing by Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.160
to like. He definitely has a pattern of this. And&nbsp;
there's witnesses like you. You definitely have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.640
a solid case here. So my sense is that if they&nbsp;
decide not to indict, it's going to be more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.520
political calculation than a criminal one. They&nbsp;
may just say, you know, we've never prosecuted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.080
a former president. We don't want to go down that&nbsp;
road. So we're going to let him off the hook here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.640
And I hope that doesn't happen. I hope that he's&nbsp;
held accountable. Also, someone who thinks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.800
Trump is going to be indicted in this Georgia case&nbsp;
is Eric Holder, who was Obama's attorney general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.080
Let's take a look at him saying that he&nbsp;
suspects an indictment is going to come soon.&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.160
Speaker 2: I think, you know, you can look at&nbsp;
what Merrick Garland is going to do. I think my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.280
primary, my first analysis would be what's going&nbsp;
to happen to him in Georgia? Well, you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:33.120
him on tape saying, you know, find me 11,780.&nbsp;
That one is close to a layup. It seems to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.520
I've always said the same thing about that. Why&nbsp;
don't they just go after that one moment. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:46.080
on. Tape. You have the smoking gun. He's saying. I&nbsp;
mean, I remember when Rod Blagojevich was on tape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.600
Basically, it didn't work out too well for him.&nbsp;
No, I know. But but it was it was it was it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.880
pretty blatant, but it wasn't as bad as find me&nbsp;
11,000 votes. Yeah. Yeah. No, I think as I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.040
I think that's close to a lay up. The D.A.&nbsp;
down there is going to be calling witnesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.880
I understand, before a special grand jury. But&nbsp;
the interesting thing there is that that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.600
also be a specification and a federal charge&nbsp;
that could be brought in Washington, D.C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.160
When you're charging him with conspiracy to&nbsp;
defraud and conspiracy to obstruct, what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.640
in Georgia can actually be part of a federal case&nbsp;
as well. So I think, you know, I was a federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.360
prosecutor doing political corruption cases when I&nbsp;
first came to the Justice Department for 12 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.760
And you get a feel for these things after a while.&nbsp;
I get the feeling that I can see where this one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.280
going and a lot of high level people in the Trump&nbsp;
administration. I suspect the president himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.600
people at the Justice Department, are all going&nbsp;
to find themselves on a little document that says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.360
the United States versus fill in the blank.
Speaker 1: So there you have it. Eric Holder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.240
suspecting that Donald Trump is going to be&nbsp;
indicted in this Georgia election fraud case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.440
And I just get the sense that if Trump is going&nbsp;
to have to face any consequences for his alleged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.480
wrongdoings, it's going to be because of this&nbsp;
Georgia case like the other investigations. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.920
just don't see them going anywhere at&nbsp;
this point. The one out of New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.920
for example, there just doesn't seem to be&nbsp;
the appetite to charge him. And we've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.480
resignations because it seems clear that they're&nbsp;
just not willing to go in that direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.640
Merrick Garland and the Biden Justice Department,&nbsp;
I just don't know if they want to open up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.400
can of worms and start this tradition of&nbsp;
presidents prosecuting former presidents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.840
Trump probably could get charged for his alleged&nbsp;
financial crimes and his role in the insurrection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.600
and the hush money payments that he had Michael&nbsp;
Cohen allegedly issue and that Cohen went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.800
jail for. But to me, this one just seems like&nbsp;
it has the highest chance of actually getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.520
an indictment, going to trial and getting a&nbsp;
conviction. So we'll see how it goes. We're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.080
to find out whether Trump will actually ever have&nbsp;
to face consequences for anything in his life.

